{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.13.18","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/runnables?scriptVersionId=366321727\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Why Runnables in LangChain? (The Why)\n\n### 1. The First Problem: Different LLM APIs\n\nWhen different LLM providers made their APIs publicly available, developers realized that the way of interacting with different LLM APIs was different.\n\nFor example, OpenAI, Google, and other LLM providers had different ways of making API calls.\n\nTo solve this problem, developers created a framework called **LangChain**, using which we could interact with different types of LLMs with very little difference in the code.\n\nThis was the first problem solved by LangChain.\n\n### 2. The Second Problem: Building an LLM Application\n\nInteracting with an LLM is just a small part of building an LLM-based application. We need many other components as well.\n\nFor example, suppose we are building a PDF reader application. We need to perform the following steps:\n\n1. Load the PDF.\n2. Perform chunking (divide the PDF into smaller chunks).\n3. Generate embeddings for those chunks.\n4. Store the embeddings in a vector store.\n5. When the user asks a query, retrieve the most relevant chunks.\n6. Send the retrieved chunks to the LLM.\n7. Parse the response.\n8. Return the final answer to the user.\n\nOver time, LangChain developed different components required to build LLM applications.\n\n### 3. The Third Problem: Connecting Different Components\n\nNow, we had different components available in LangChain.\n\nHowever, developers had to write code manually to connect one component with another.\n\nTo make developers' lives easier, LangChain introduced **Chains**.\n\nChains allowed developers to connect multiple components together to create a workflow.\n\nThe most basic and oldest type of chain was `LLMChain`, which takes an LLM and a Prompt Template as input.\n\nOver time, LangChain introduced many different chain functions for different use cases.\n\n### 4. Problems with Having Too Many Chain Functions\n\nAs LangChain evolved, it introduced so many chain functions that two major problems started appearing:\n\n1. **Large codebase:** Maintaining such a large codebase became difficult.\n2. **Complex learning curve:** Learning LangChain became increasingly difficult because developers had to understand so many different chain functions.\n\n### 5. But Why Did LangChain Need So Many Different Chains?\n\nThe main problem was that all the components of LangChain were not standardized.\n\nBy standardization, we mean that different components behaved differently and had different methods for performing their operations.\n\nFor example:\n\n- To call an LLM, we had to use the `predict()` function.\n- To format a prompt, we had to use the `format()` function.\n\nSince different components behaved differently, LangChain developers had to write custom code to connect one component to another.\n\nThis was one of the reasons why LangChain needed so many different chain functions.\n\n### 6. The Solution: Runnables\n\nLater, the LangChain team realized that they needed to standardize all the components.\n\nThis was achieved through **Runnables**.\n\nRunnables introduced a common interface that different LangChain components could follow.\n\nAs a result, components could be connected more easily without writing separate custom integration code for every combination.\n\nThis made LangChain:\n\n- Easier to use.\n- Easier to maintain.\n- Easier to learn.\n- More flexible when connecting different components.\n\n**This is the reason behind the introduction of Runnables in LangChain.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Runnables in LangChain\n\n## 1. What is a Runnable?\n\nA **Runnable is a unit of work in LangChain that performs a specific task.**\n\nEvery Runnable has a particular purpose. We provide it with an input, it processes that input, and produces an output.\n\nFor example:\n- PromptTemplate: Takes input variables and generates a formatted prompt.\n- Chat Model: Takes a prompt and generates an AI response.\n- Output Parser: Takes the model's response and converts it into the required format.\n- Retriever: Takes a query and retrieves relevant documents.\n\nEach of these components can act as a Runnable.\n\n## 2. Characteristics of Runnables\n\n### 1. Every Runnable performs a specific task\n\nEvery Runnable has a particular responsibility.\n\nWe give it an input, it processes that input, and produces an output.\n\n### 2. Common Interface\n\nEvery Runnable follows a common interface, which means different Runnables provide a common set of methods to perform their operations.\n\nSome important methods are:\n\n- `invoke()`: Executes a Runnable with a single input.\n- `batch()`: Executes a Runnable with multiple inputs.\n- `stream()`: Produces output progressively instead of waiting for the complete output.\n- `ainvoke()`: Executes a Runnable asynchronously.\n\nBecause Runnables follow a common interface, we can work with different components in a similar way.\n\n### 3. Composition of Runnables\n\nWe can connect one Runnable to another to create a workflow.\n\nThe important thing is that **when we connect multiple Runnables together, the resulting workflow also becomes a Runnable.**\n\nThis means we can treat the entire workflow just like an individual Runnable.\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\nchain = prompt | model | parser\n```\n\nHere:\n- `prompt` is a Runnable.\n- `model` is a Runnable.\n- `parser` is a Runnable.\n- The complete `chain` is also a Runnable.\n\nWe can execute the entire workflow using:\n\n```python\nresult = chain.invoke({\"topic\": \"Cricket\"})\n```\n\n## 3. How do Runnables work together?\n\nConsider the following workflow:\n\n```text\n        User Input\n             |\n             v\n       PromptTemplate\n         (Runnable)\n             |\n             v\n        Chat Model\n         (Runnable)\n             |\n             v\n      Output Parser\n         (Runnable)\n             |\n             v\n        Final Output\n```\n\nEach Runnable performs its own task, and its output is passed to the next Runnable.\n\nThe complete connected workflow is also treated as a single Runnable.\n\n## 4. Advantages of Runnables\n\n1. **Standardization:** All Runnables follow a common interface.\n2. **Reusability:** We can reuse individual Runnables in different workflows.\n3. **Easy composition:** We can easily connect different components to create complex workflows.\n4. **Simpler code:** We don't need to write separate integration code for every combination of components.\n5. **Multiple execution methods:** We can use invoke, batch, stream, and asynchronous methods.\n\n## 5. Important Takeaway\n\nRunnables standardized the way different LangChain components work and interact with each other.\n\nInstead of having different methods and custom integration code for every component, we now have a common interface.\n\nAlso, a workflow created by connecting multiple Runnables is itself a Runnable.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n# HOW\n\n## 1. Creation of the Runnable Class\n\nTo standardize all the components of LangChain, the developers created a common class called **Runnable**.\n\n- Runnable is an abstract base class.\n- It defines a common interface that other components must follow.\n- Other LangChain components inherit from this Runnable class (directly or through intermediate classes).\n\nFor example:\n- PromptTemplate\n- Chat Models\n- Output Parsers\n- Retrievers\n\nThese components perform different tasks, but they follow the same Runnable interface.\n\n### What is an Abstract Base Class?\n\nAn abstract base class is a class that acts as a blueprint for other classes.\n\n- We generally don't create objects directly from an abstract class.\n- It defines the structure and common behavior that its child classes should follow.\n- Child classes implement the required functionality.\n\nIn our case, Runnable acts as a common blueprint for different LangChain components.\n\n## 2. How Does Standardization Work?\n\nBefore Runnables, different components had different methods for performing their operations.\n\nFor example:\n- LLM had its own methods.\n- Prompt Template had its own methods.\n- Other components also had their own methods.\n\nNow, the Runnable class provides a common set of methods that different components can use.\n\nSome important methods are:\n\n- `invoke()`: Takes a single input, processes it and returns an output.\n- `batch()`: Processes multiple inputs.\n- `stream()`: Returns output progressively.\n- `ainvoke()`: Performs asynchronous execution.\n\nDifferent components can implement or override these methods according to their requirements.\n\n**Result:** We can interact with different LangChain components using a common interface instead of learning different execution methods for every component.\n\n## 3. Creation of a Runnable Connector Class\n\nNow we have standardized individual components, but we also need to connect them together.\n\nFor this purpose, LangChain provides a composition class called **RunnableSequence**.\n\nRunnableSequence is responsible for connecting multiple Runnables in a sequence.\n\nIts job is to:\n- Take multiple Runnable components.\n- Execute them in a predefined order.\n- Pass the output of one Runnable as the input to the next Runnable.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPromptTemplate → Chat Model → Output Parser\n```\n\nHere, the output of PromptTemplate goes to Chat Model, and the output of Chat Model goes to Output Parser.\n\n## 4. How Does the Pipe (|) Operator Work?\n\nLangChain allows us to use the `|` operator to connect multiple Runnables.\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\nchain = prompt | model | parser\n```\n\nInternally, this composition creates a RunnableSequence.\n\nThe important thing is:\n\n- Prompt is a Runnable.\n- Model is a Runnable.\n- Parser is a Runnable.\n- RunnableSequence connects these components.\n- RunnableSequence is also a Runnable.\n\nThis is possible because RunnableSequence itself follows the Runnable interface.\n\nTherefore, the complete workflow can also use methods such as `invoke()`, `batch()` and `stream()`.\n\n## 5. How Does the Complete Architecture Work?\n\n1. LangChain created an abstract base class called Runnable.\n2. Different components follow this common Runnable interface through inheritance.\n3. Every component provides a common set of execution methods.\n4. LangChain provides RunnableSequence to connect multiple Runnables.\n5. The pipe (`|`) operator creates a RunnableSequence.\n6. Since RunnableSequence is also a Runnable, the complete connected workflow can be treated as a single Runnable.\n\n## 6. Important Takeaway\n\nThe main purpose of Runnables is to standardize LangChain components and make their composition easier.\n\nInstead of writing custom integration code to connect different components, we can use a common interface and connect them using the pipe operator.\n\n**Remember these two important classes:**\n\n| Class | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Runnable | Abstract base class that defines the common interface |\n| RunnableSequence | Connects multiple Runnables sequentially |\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Categories of LangChain Runnables\n\nLangChain Runnables can be broadly divided into two categories:\n\n1. **Task-Specific Runnables**\n2. **Runnable Primitives**\n\n## 1. Task-Specific Runnables\n\n**Definition:** These are core LangChain components that have been adapted to follow the Runnable interface so that they can be used in pipelines and composed with other Runnables.\n\n**Purpose:** They perform specific operations required in an LLM application, such as prompting, calling an LLM, and retrieving relevant documents.\n\n**Examples:**\n\n- **ChatOpenAI:** Communicates with an OpenAI chat model and generates responses.\n- **PromptTemplate:** Creates and formats prompts dynamically using input variables.\n- **Retriever:** Retrieves relevant documents based on a given query.\n\nIn short, Task-Specific Runnables are responsible for performing the actual operations in an LLM workflow.\n\nDefinition: These are core LangChain components that have been converted into Runnables so they can be used in pipelines.\n\nPurpose: Perform task-specific operations like LLM calls, prompting, retrieval, etc.\n\n## 2. Runnable Primitives\n\n**Definition:** Runnable Primitives are fundamental building blocks used to structure and control the execution of Runnables in an AI workflow.\n\n**Purpose:** They determine how multiple Runnables interact and execute. For example, they can execute steps sequentially, in parallel, or conditionally.\n\nDefinition: These are fundamental building blocks for structuring execution logic in AI workflows.\n\nPurpose: They help orchestrate execution by defining how different Runnables interact (sequentially, in parallel, conditionally, etc.).\n\n**Examples:**\n\n### 1. RunnableSequence\n- Executes multiple Runnables one after another, in a specific order.\n- The output of one Runnable becomes the input of the next Runnable.\n- The pipe (`|`) operator is commonly used to create a sequence.\n\n### 2. RunnableParallel\n- Executes multiple Runnables simultaneously.\n- All Runnables receive the same input.\n- Their outputs are collected into a dictionary.\n\n### 3. RunnableMap\n- Applies multiple operations to the same input.\n- In LangChain, `RunnableMap` is commonly used as another name for `RunnableParallel`.\n\n### 4. RunnableBranch\n- Supports conditional execution (if-else logic).\n- Selects and executes a Runnable based on the condition that matches the input.\n\n### 5. RunnableLambda\n- Converts a custom Python function into a Runnable.\n- Allows us to integrate our own Python logic into a Runnable pipeline.\n\n### 6. RunnablePassthrough\n- Passes the input directly to the output without modifying it.\n- Useful when we want to preserve or forward the original input within a pipeline.\n\n## Key Difference\n\n| Task-Specific Runnables | Runnable Primitives |\n|---|---|\n| Perform specific operations. | Control how operations are executed. |\n| Examples: ChatOpenAI, PromptTemplate, Retriever. | Examples: RunnableSequence, RunnableParallel, RunnableBranch. |\n| Focus on *what task needs to be performed*. | Focus on *how the workflow should execute*. |\n\n**Remember:** Task-Specific Runnables perform the work, while Runnable Primitives help organize and coordinate that work.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# LCEL (LangChain Expression Language)\n\n## What is LCEL?\n\nLCEL stands for **LangChain Expression Language**.\n\nIt is a way of connecting multiple LangChain components (Runnables) to create a complete workflow or chain.\n\nUsing LCEL, we can connect components such as:\n- PromptTemplate\n- Chat Model\n- Output Parser\n- Retriever\n\nIt allows us to build chains without writing separate code to connect the output of one component to the input of another.\n\n## What is the Pipe (`|`) Operator?\n\nThe pipe (`|`) operator is used in LCEL to connect multiple Runnables together.\n\nIt connects the output of one Runnable to the input of the next Runnable.\n\n**How does it work?**\n\nSuppose we have three components:\n\n1. PromptTemplate\n2. Chat Model\n3. Output Parser\n\nWe connect them using the pipe operator:\n\n`prompt | model | parser`\n\nThe execution happens in the following order:\n\n1. **PromptTemplate:** Takes the user's input and creates a properly formatted prompt.\n2. **Chat Model:** Receives the formatted prompt and generates an AI response.\n3. **Output Parser:** Takes the model's response and converts it into the required output format.\n\nThe final output is returned after all three components have executed.\n\n## Important Points\n\n- The pipe operator connects Runnables in a sequence.\n- The output of one Runnable becomes the input of the next Runnable.\n- The order of components matters because they execute from left to right.\n- The complete pipeline is also a Runnable.\n- We can execute the complete pipeline using methods such as `invoke()`.\n\n## What Happens Internally?\n\nWhen we use the pipe (`|`) operator to connect Runnables, LangChain creates a **RunnableSequence**.\n\nRunnableSequence is responsible for executing the connected Runnables one after another.\n\nFor example:\n\n`prompt | model | parser`\n\nThis creates a RunnableSequence containing the prompt, model, and parser.\n\n**Remember:**\n- LCEL is the way we define and compose workflows.\n- The pipe (`|`) operator connects Runnables.\n- RunnableSequence executes those connected Runnables in order. ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Pipe (`|`) Operator in LCEL\n\nThe pipe (`|`) operator is used to connect multiple Runnables sequentially and create a **RunnableSequence**.\n\nIt passes the output of one Runnable as the input to the next Runnable.\n\n**Important points:**\n\n1. The pipe operator is used to create a sequential workflow (`RunnableSequence`).\n2. It does not define every type of Runnable Primitive.\n3. Other primitives, such as `RunnableParallel` and `RunnableBranch`, have their own ways of defining execution logic.\n4. A `RunnableSequence` can contain other Runnables, including a `RunnableBranch` or `RunnableParallel`.\n5. The complete sequence is also a Runnable and can be executed using methods such as `invoke()`.\n\n**Remember:** The pipe operator is used to compose a sequence, not to create every type of Runnable Primitive.","metadata":{}}]}